on-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "57ce164a08af750120cbd68f267e801bdb796faaf43d21787f7e23c7491a1041" "basis" 836 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-11007" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-22x-59}{({x}^{2}+11)(x-5)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+11)}+\\frac{C}{(x-5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+11$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+11)(x-5)$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-22x-59=(Ax+B)(x-5)+C({x}^{2}+11).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=5$ eliminates $A$ and $B$ and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2}-22(5)-59 & =(A(5)+B)(5-5)+C({5}^{2}+11) \\\\ 25-110-59 & =36C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{144}{36} \\\\ C & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}-22(0)-59 & =(A(0)+B)(0-5)+C({0}^{2}+11) \\\\ -59 & =-5B+11C \\\\ -59 & =-5B+11(-4) \\\\ 5B & =-44+59 \\\\ B & =\\frac{15}{5} \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}-22(1)-59 & =(A(1)+B)(1-5)+C({1}^{2}+11) \\\\ 1-22-59 & =-4A-4B+12C \\\\ -80 & =-4A-4(3)+12(-4) \\\\ 4A & =-12-48+80 \\\\ A & =\\frac{20}{4} \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=5,$ $B=3,$ and $C=-4.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-22x-59}{({x}^{2}+11)(x-5)}=\\frac{5x+3}{({x}^{2}+11)}-\\frac{4}{(x-5)}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "57ce164a08af750120cbd68f267e801bdb796faaf43d21787f7e23c7491a1041" "basis" 836 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/