e" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6{x}^{2}-8x+5}{({x}^{2}+x+3)(x-4)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+x+3)}+\\frac{C}{(x-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+2x+3$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+x+3)(x-4)$ and get\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-8x+5=(Ax+B)(x-4)+C({x}^{2}+x+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(4)^{2}-8(4)+5 & =(A(4)+B)(4-4)+C({4}^{2}+4+3) \\\\ 69 & =23C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(0)^{2}-8(0)+5 & =(A(0)+B)(0-4)+C({0}^{2}+0+3) \\\\ 5 & =-4B+3C \\\\ 5 & =-4B+3(3) \\\\ 4B & =9-5 \\\\ B & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(1)^{2}-8(1)+5 & =(A(1)+B)(1-4)+C({1}^{2}+1+3) \\\\ 3 & =-3A-3B+5C \\\\ 3 & =-3A-3(1)+5(3) \\\\ 3 & =-3A-3+15 \\\\ 3A & =-3-3+15 \\\\ A & =\\frac{9}{3} \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=3,B=1,$ and $C=3.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{6{x}^{2}-8x+5}{({x}^{2}+x+3)(x-4)}=\\frac{3x+1}{({x}^{2}+x+3)}+\\frac{3}{(x-4)}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038841/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf711482421f54e9c818febf4b0afb6df8de8acfcaa9776a38291db587e391ab"} {"question" "q-102575" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038841/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ed8fcb99e086b7bf84ce4fbb31e5c955e257fb26c249ca1c04fbe58284e6a0c0"} {"question" "q-102575" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038841/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf711482421f54e9c818febf4b0afb6df8de8acfcaa9776a38291db587e391ab"} {"question" "q-102575" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2x+1}{({x}^{2}+x+3)}-\\frac{5}{(x-4)}"] ["math" "\\frac{2x+3}{({x}^{2}+x+3)}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)}"] ["math" "\\frac{3x+1}{({x}^{2}+x+3)}+\\frac{3}{(x-4)}"] ["math" "\\frac{3x+4}{({x}^{2}+x+3)}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)}"] ["math" "\\frac{x-2}{({x}^{2}+x+3)}+\\frac{3}{(x-4)}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038841/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf711482421f54e9c818febf4b0afb6df8de8acfcaa9776a38291db587e391ab"} {"question" "q-102575" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3x+1}{({x}^{2}+x+3)}+\\frac{3}{(x-4)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038841/state.json" "file_sha256" "bf711482421f54e9c818febf4b0afb6df8de8acfcaa9776a38291db587e391ab"} {"question" "e-4046" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7 x^2 - 2x - 13 }{(x^2+1)(x+1) } = \\dfrac{9x+B}{ x^2 +1} - \\dfrac{2}{x+1} ,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $B?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038841/example-4046.json" "file_sha256" "546288c5395aac6674be99161124d8ca6e329d01641f1f7cc8826f8286414621"} {"question" "e-4046" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x^2+1)(x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}^{2}-2x-13 & =(9x+B)(x+1)-2({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(0)^{2}-2(0)-13 & =(9\\cdot (0)+B)(0+1)-2((0)^{2}+1) \\\\ -13 & =B-2 \\\\ B & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038841/example-4046.json" "file_sha256" "546288c5395aac6674be99161124d8ca6e329d01641f1f7cc8826f8286414621"} {"question" "e-504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Express $\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x^2+4)(x-1)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038841/example-504.json" "file_sha256" "2ba95bdcf52b9b8e9017d77dffd6a91162c117a58aa359920e78ac11b9f6a8a9"} {"question" "e-504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{C}{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since $x^2+4$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $(x^2+4)(x-1)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =(Ax+B)(x-1)+C({x}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)+3 & =(A(1)+B)(1-1)+C({1}^{2}+4) \\\\ 5 & =5C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)+3 & =(A(0)+B)(0-1)+C({0}^{2}+4) \\\\ 3 & =-B+4C \\\\ 3 & =-B+4(1) \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=2$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)+3 & =(A(2)+B)(2-1)+C({2}^{2}+4) \\\\ 7 & =2A+B+8C \\\\ 7 & =2A+1+8(1) \\\\ 2A & =-2 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=-1,$ $B=1,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x^2+4)(x-1)} = \\dfrac{1-x}{x^2+4} +\\dfrac{1}{x-1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038841/example-504.json" "file_sha256" "2ba95bdcf52b9b8e9017d77dffd6a91162c117a58aa359920e78ac11b9f6a8a9"}] "usage" {"e-4046" [] "q-11007" [] "q-102561" [] "q-49718" [] "q-11014" [] "q-11015" [] "q-102560" [] "q-346957" [] "e-504" []} "basis" 1341 "retractions" [[17592186097204 :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that\n$(5)/((x^{2} + 1)(x + 2)) = \\frac{Ax + B}{x^{2} + 1} + \\frac{C}{x + 2}$,\nwhere $A$, $B$ and $C$ are real constants, determine the value of $A + B + C$."] [17592186096353 :question/problem "Given that\n$\\dfrac{7 x^2 - 2x - 13}{(x^2+1)(x+1)} = \\dfrac{9x+B}{x^2 +1} - \\dfrac{2}{x+1}$,\n\nwhat is the value of $B$?"] [17592186096353 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x^2+1)(x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x^{2} - 2x - 13 &= (9x + B)(x + 1) - 2(x^{2} + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7(0)^{2} - 2(0) - 13 &= (9 \\cdot (0) + B)(0 + 1) - 2((0)^{2} + 1) \\\\\n- 13 &= B - 2 \\\\\nB &= -11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186096411 :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A$, $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2x + 3)/((x^{2} + 4)(x - 1)) &= \\frac{Ax + B}{x^{2} + 4} + \\frac{C}{x - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since $x^2+4$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $(x^2+4)(x-1)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 3 &= (Ax + B)(x - 1) + C(x^{2} + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(1) + 3 &= (A(1) + B)(1 - 1) + C(1^{2} + 4) \\\\\n5 &= 5C \\\\\nC &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(0) + 3 &= (A(0) + B)(0 - 1) + C(0^{2} + 4) \\\\\n3 &= -B + 4C \\\\\n3 &= -B + 4(1) \\\\\nB &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A$, we choose $x=2$ and use our results for $B$ and $C$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(2) + 3 &= (A(2) + B)(2 - 1) + C(2^{2} + 4) \\\\\n7 &= 2A + B + 8C \\\\\n7 &= 2A + 1 + 8(1) \\\\\n2A &= -2 \\\\\nA &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=-1$, $B=1$, and $C=1$. Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x^2+4)(x-1)} = \\dfrac{1-x}{x^2+4} +\\dfrac{1}{x-1}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186093622 17592186093623 17592186093624 17592186093625 17592186093626 17592186095582 17592186095583 17592186095584 17592186095585 17592186095586 17592186266451 17592186266452 17592186266453 17592186266454 17592186266455 17592186266479 17592186266480 17592186266481 17592186266482 17592186266483 17592186266486 17592186266488 17592186266490 17592186278739 17592186278740 17592186278741 17592186278742 17592186278743 17592186278746 17592186278747 17592186278748 17592186278749 17592186278750] "immutable_fields" [[17592186093621 :answer-field/correct] [17592186093621 :answer-field/id] [17592186093621 :answer-field/key] [17592186093621 :answer-field/type] [17592186095581 :answer-field/correct] [17592186095581 :answer-field/id] [17592186095581 :answer-field/key] [17592186095581 :answer-field/type] [17592186266450 :answer-field/correct] [17592186266450 :answer-field/id] [17592186266450 :answer-field/key] [17592186266450 :answer-field/type] [17592186266478 :answer-field/correct] [17592186266478 :answer-field/id] [17592186266478 :answer-field/key] [17592186266478 :answer-field/type] [17592186266485 :answer-field/correct] [17592186266485 :answer-field/id] [17592186266485 :answer-field/key] [17592186266485 :answer-field/type] [17592186266487 :answer-field/correct] [17592186266487 :answer-field/id] [17592186266487 :answer-field/key] [17592186266487 :answ